   "sources": [
    {
     "label": "Nelson, G.D. & Rae, A. (2016) An Economic Geography of the United States: From Commutes to Megaregions. PLOS ONE 11(11): e0166083",
     "url": "https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0166083"
    },
    {
     "label": "Data: Rae & Nelson, United States Commutes and Megaregions data for GIS (mosaic-regions), Univ. of Sheffield Figshare, doi:10.15131/shef.data.4110156.v5, CC BY 4.0",
     "url": "https://doi.org/10.15131/shef.data.4110156"
    },
    {
     "label": "Counties: US Census Bureau 2023 cartographic boundaries",
     "url": "https://www.census.gov/geographies/mapping-files/time-series/geo/cartographic-boundary.html"
    }
   ],
   "notes": "Method: the authors took 4.16 million tract-to-tract commuter flows (ACS 2006–2010), ran the Combo community-detection algorithm to group tracts that commute to each other, then refined the result visually into megaregions (Fig. 11 of the paper). This map uses the authors' official tract-built region file (mosaic-regions, detailed) and assigns each 2023 county to the region covering most of its land area (computed in equal-area projection); 49 counties are split across regions with under 60% in one. Region names are the paper's (‘Dalls Ft Worth’ spelling corrected); two regions unnamed in the paper use the authors' later names (Sacagawea, Big Sky), and the later alternative names are in each popup. Alaska and Hawaii were not part of the study. Colours are a 4-colour scheme so neighbouring regions never share a colour; they carry no meaning."
  },
