   "notes": "Routes scheduled on the Airbus A380 for the Northern winter 2026/27 season (25 Oct 2026 – 27 Mar 2027), as of November 2026. One line per airline route (both directions). Data accessed 2 Nov 2026. Frequencies are weekly A380 flights each way from airline schedule filings (AeroRoutes, MainlyMiles, ANA). Where no current count was published (most Emirates routes) the field says 'unknown' and a January 2026 reference count from Flightradar24 is shown instead; circle sizes use that reference, so they are approximate. Block times are only given where a source published them (Singapore Airlines). Distances are great-circle from OurAirports coordinates. Not shown: Emirates Dubai–Washington (777-300ER this winter per AeroRoutes, although Cirium/BI still listed it), Qantas Melbourne–Los Angeles (A380 removed 24 Oct 2026), Lufthansa Munich–Boston/Denver/Mumbai (summer only). British Airways London–Johannesburg is shown but sources conflict (see route note). GEEK NOTES — Active A380s: Emirates 116 in fleet (85 active Sep 2026), BA 12, Singapore 12, Qantas 10, Etihad 9, Lufthansa 8, Qatar 8, Asiana 6, Korean Air 5, ANA 3 (Wikipedia, Jun 2026; ch-aviation via BI). Longest A380 route: Emirates Dubai–Auckland, 14,199 km (8,823 mi). Shortest: Asiana Seoul–Tokyo Narita, 1,257 km (781 mi). Busiest A380 airport: Dubai (about 550 weekly A380 departures, using the Jan 2026 reference counts), then London Heathrow and Singapore."
